  "text": {
    "work": "work",
    "card": "Turn a crank pin inside an open slot to index a straight-guided yoke.",
    "scotch_yoke": "Scotch yoke",
    "pin_slot": "sliding pin and transverse slot",
    "wrong_phase": "Choose the turn matching the current phase.",
    "free_hand": "Put down an object so a hand is free for the rear crank control.",
    "howto_turn": "Use the next or previous offer matching the current phase at the rear purple shaft control. Blue C is the crank, orange P the same original pin and green Y the translating closed-frame yoke. The genuine vertical slot is transverse to horizontal yoke travel. Next turns counterclockwise viewed from the front, in sixteen 22.5 degree indexed endpoints; phase 0 has the pin at the right. A 120 mm crank carries the pin around the shaft, while rounded 120 cos(theta) moves the whole yoke through 240 mm along two fixed guides. The pale yoke mark travels between two mounted red endpoint marks; four rear red cardinal references stay fixed. Open sleeve bores remain engaged with the guide shafts throughout the stroke. The original pin spans crank and slot depth planes; its wider retaining head remains ahead of the slot walls. This pin-slot pair has no rigid connecting rod or cam follower. Only sixteen endpoint configurations are depicted: no continuous swept clearance, sinusoidal interpolation, autonomous motion, physical force, friction, power conversion, output, load, loose-item custody, structural restraint or engine collision/support change is simulated."
  },
